<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#green-corn-ceremony">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Green Corn Ceremony</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Busk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Green Corn Ceremony is the most important annual festival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> (Creek), Seminole and other peoples of the south-eastern woodlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Held in midsummer when the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> ripens, it is a rite of renewal: fires are extinguished and relit, offences forgiven, and dances, fasting and purification take place at the town's square ground. It remains central to traditional communities today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/green-corn-ceremony.jpg" alt="Green Corn Ceremony" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Catlin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Green_Corn_Ceremony_-_Minatarrees.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
